/*
 * LC 217 — Contains Duplicate
 *
 * PROBLEM:
 * Given an integer array nums, return true if any value appears
 * at least twice, false if every element is distinct.
 *
 * CONSTRAINTS:
 * - 1 <= nums.length <= 10^5
 * - -10^9 <= nums[i] <= 10^9
 * - Negatives possible → boolean array indexing won't work
 * - Length up to 100k → O(n²) is 10^10 ops → too slow
 *
 * ALL APPROACHES:
 * ┌──────────────┬─────────────────────────┬──────────┬──────┐
 * │ Approach     │ Idea                    │ TC       │ SC   │
 * ├──────────────┼─────────────────────────┼──────────┼──────┤
 * │ Brute Force  │ Compare every pair      │ O(n²)    │ O(1) │
 * │ Sorting      │ Sort → check adjacent   │ O(nlogn) │ O(1) │
 * │ HashSet      │ Existence check         │ O(n)     │ O(n) │
 * └──────────────┴─────────────────────────┴──────────┴──────┘
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────
 * APPROACH 1 — BRUTE FORCE
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────
 * WHY: Simplest possible — compare every element with every other.
 * HOW: Two nested loops. For each i, scan all j > i.
 *      If nums[i] == nums[j] → duplicate found.
 *
 * REJECTED BECAUSE:
 * O(n²) — for n=10^5 that's 10^10 comparisons. TLE.
 *
 * TC: O(n²) — n*(n-1)/2 comparisons worst case
 * SC: O(1)  — no extra space
 *
 * Best case:  O(1) — duplicate at index 0,1
 * Worst case: O(n²) — no duplicate or duplicate at end
 */
public boolean containsDuplicateBrute(int[] nums) {
    for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
        for (int j = i + 1; j < nums.length; j++) {
            // compare every pair — if equal, duplicate found
            if (nums[i] == nums[j]) return true;
        }
    }
    return false;
}

/*
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────
 * APPROACH 2 — SORTING (Intermediate)
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────
 * WHY: Key insight — if duplicates exist, after sorting they
 *      MUST be adjacent. No need to compare all pairs.
 * HOW: Sort array → single pass checking nums[i] == nums[i+1].
 *
 * KEY OBSERVATION:
 * [1, 3, 1, 2] → sort → [1, 1, 2, 3]
 *                              ↑↑ adjacent duplicate visible
 *
 * TRADEOFF vs Optimal:
 * - Better than brute: O(n log n) vs O(n²)
 * - Worse than HashSet: O(n log n) vs O(n)
 * - MUTATES input array — Arrays.sort() is in-place
 *   If original order must be preserved, need to clone first → O(n) SC
 * - No early exit during sort phase
 *
 * TC: O(n log n) — dominated by sort; single pass is O(n)
 * SC: O(1)       — in-place sort (ignoring call stack)
 *                  O(n) if input must be preserved (clone needed)
 *
 * Best case:  O(n log n) — no early exit during sort
 * Worst case: O(n log n) — same
 */
public boolean containsDuplicateSorting(int[] nums) {
    // sort so duplicates become adjacent
    Arrays.sort(nums);
    // single pass — only need to check neighbors
    for (int i = 0; i < nums.length - 1; i++) {
        // after sorting, any duplicate must be at i and i+1
        if (nums[i] == nums[i + 1]) return true;
    }
    return false;
}

/*
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────
 * APPROACH 3 — HASHSET (Optimal)
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────
 * WHY HashSet:
 * We need O(1) existence check — "have we seen this number before?"
 * HashSet gives O(1) average contains() and add() via hashing.
 * No mutation of input. No nested loops.
 *
 * HOW:
 * Single pass. For each number:
 *   - Already in set? → duplicate → return true immediately
 *   - Not in set?     → add and continue
 * Loop completes without hit → no duplicates → return false
 *
 * MISCONCEPTION CLEARED:
 * contains() and add() both compute hash → find bucket → walk chain.
 * contains() stops there. add() additionally inserts if not found.
 * Both are O(1) average. contains() slightly cheaper in found-case.
 * No meaningful TC difference between them at Big-O level.
 *
 * WHY NOT boolean array:
 * nums[i] range is -10^9 to 10^9 — can't use as array index directly.
 * Would need offset of 10^9 → array of size 2*10^9 → memory explosion.
 *
 * TC: O(n)  — single pass, O(1) per contains/add
 * SC: O(n)  — worst case all n elements in set before duplicate found
 *
 * Best case:  O(1) — duplicate at index 0 and 1
 * Worst case: O(n) — duplicate at last two positions or no duplicate
 *
 * Edge cases:
 * - Single element → loop runs once → returns false ✅
 * - All same elements → returns true on index 1 ✅
 * - Negatives handled — HashSet works with any Integer value ✅
 */
public boolean containsDuplicate(int[] nums) {
    Set<Integer> set = new HashSet<>();
    for (int num : nums) {
        // O(1) check — hash finds bucket, equals() finds key in chain
        if (set.contains(num)) return true;
        // O(1) insert — only reached if num not seen before
        set.add(num);
    }
    return false;
}
